;;; A theory built on a theory, imported by an entry that does not name the
;;; lower one's: the witness that nothing is left behind, since every import
;;; brings its module whole, with the module's own imports (Q-M1f.247).
;;;
;;; `upper_without_entry.ein` imports `lower` by its entry and uses it; its
;;; own entry, `upper`, names its two rules and not `lower`. Were an import
;;; to keep only the names it lists and what they reach, `(upper)` would
;;; bring `lower`'s declarations and none of its rules, and `(ask a)` would
;;; be derived and never answered; the goal's row is what says the lower
;;; theory came.
;;;
;;; Every import brings its module whole, with the module's own imports, and
;;; the import forms differ only in names (Q-M1f.247, Q-M1f.249):
;;; `upper_without_entry`'s own import of `lower` is an import of the
;;; program, so the lower theory is here and answers, as it does for
;;; `01_nested_import_whole.ein`, and a selection only names. The program
;;; lists what it writes, `wanted` and `got`. An import under an alias,
;;; `(import upper_without_entry :as u)`, gives the same answer
;;; (Q-M1f.249). It states no hypothesis, to
;;; keep the answer small: R {∅}, and `a`'s double is `aa`.

(import upper_without_entry :symbols (upper wanted got))

(wanted a)

(solve :hypothesis-relations () :expect (region (model)))
(query :goal (got ?x ?y)
       :goal-text "{?x}'s double is {?y}"
       :expect (model (got a aa)))
